However, the issue I identified earlier is still valid. In the proxy.go ServeHTTP function, when s.user.allowCORS is true, the code unconditionally sets the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header, which overwrites any existing CORS headers from the backend.

Let me create a fix for this issue. The fix should check if the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header is already set by the backend before setting it.

```bash
cat <<'EOF' > proxy_cors_fix.go
package main

import (
	"net/http"
)

// FixCORSHeaders checks if CORS headers are already set by the backend
// and only sets them if they're not already present
func FixCORSHeaders(s *scope, rw http.ResponseWriter, req *http.Request) {
	if s.user.allowCORS {
		// Check if Access-Control-Allow-Origin is already set by the backend
		if rw.Header().Get("Access-Control-Allow-Origin") == "" {
			origin := req.Header.Get("Origin")
			if len(origin) == 0 {
				origin = "*"
			}
			rw.Header().Set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin)
		}
	}
}
EOF
```

